Section: C: Classes and class hierarchies

Subsection: C.ctor: Constructors

Rule: C.42: If a constructor cannot construct a valid object, throw an exception

Reason: Leaving behind an invalid object is asking for trouble.

Example: class X2 {
 FILE* f;
 // ...
public:
 X2(const string& name)
 :f{fopen(name.c_str(), "r")}
 {
 if (!f) throw runtime_error{"could not open" + name};
 // ...
 }

 void read(); // read from f
 // ...
};

void f()
{
 X2 file {"Zeno"}; // throws if file isn't open
 file.read(); // fine
 // ...
}

